## Why it matters

Businesses depend on predictable schedules, auditable changes, and recoverable failures. Workflows that live only in a no-code tool or a growing script are hard to review, test, or roll back, and calling an LLM from them adds unpredictability. This framework is for teams who want AI in their automation without losing that control.

## How it works

The framework rests on three ideas.

### Determinism

Wrap LLM calls in typed steps and keep orchestration deterministic. The orchestrator decides what runs and in what order, and each agent returns output that matches a typed contract.

### Microservice agents

Each agent does one task and is deployable independently, so you can change, test, or replace one agent without touching the rest.

### Git as the source of truth

Every change is reviewed, versioned, and reversible, because agents are deployed from git.

## When to use it

Use this framework when your automation must be reviewable, repeatable, and recoverable, and when you want AI steps to behave like any other part of your codebase. For a simple, stable, linear workflow, a no-code tool is usually enough.

| | No-code workflow | Agentic framework |
|---|---|---|
| Version control | None | Git history and rollback |
| Structure | Steps inside a tool | Orchestrator plus single-purpose agents |
| Setup effort | Low | Higher, because you write and deploy code |

